    {
        "user": "U02DXRYCD2N",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1671216683.420869",
        "client_msg_id": "FE8D28B7-3BD1-4625-8768-6CA711D39448",
        "text": "The headline definitely jumped out to me as well. The line “developer dirty bomb” must have been from the hearing yesterday bc that wasn’t included in my reporting. It’s definitely an eye grabber. \n\nIt may not be sexy, but to me what’s missing from the piece is any acknowledgement of the planning department’s efforts to include community voices much more than in the past (they did do a lot of outreach and took some feedback into consideration, but this article only mentions that the housing plan includes language on race and equity). Every time I spoke with an activist and much of the time when those activists were speaking at city hall, they started by acknowledging and thanking the department for its engagement on the topic. This piece makes it sound like the department put in zero effort (or at least that’s how it reads to me). \n\nI’m not defending the housing plan or saying it’s perfect, but I worry sometimes that when groups try to be more equitable\/focus on racial justice and then feel overly criticized they abandon those goals altogether because they feel the efforts are thankless. But I suppose that would be an issue for the planning department to work through if they truly are committed to those goals. Anyways, that’s where my brain went ",
        "team": "T036YAYMK",
        "user_team": "T036YAYMK",
        "source_team": "T036YAYMK",
        "user_profile": {
            "avatar_hash": "0d799cdb03cc",
            "image_72": "https:\/\/avatars.slack-edge.com\/2025-08-29\/9437966570916_0d799cdb03ccb319f0e3_72.png",
            "first_name": "Madison",
            "real_name": "Madison Alvarado",
            "display_name": "Madison Alvarado",
            "team": "T036YAYMK",
            "name": "madison_alvarado",
            "is_restricted": false,
            "is_ultra_restricted": false
        },
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "70l",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "The headline definitely jumped out to me as well. The line “developer dirty bomb” must have been from the hearing yesterday bc that wasn’t included in my reporting. It’s definitely an eye grabber. \n\n"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "It may not be sexy, but "
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "to me what’s missing from the piece is any acknowledgement of the planning department’s efforts to include community voices much more than in the past (they did do a lot of outreach and took some feedback into consideration, but this article only mentions that the housing plan includes language on race and equity). Every time I spoke with an activist and much of the time when those activists were speaking at city hall, they started by acknowledging and thanking the department for its engagement on the topic. This piece makes it sound like the department put in zero effort (or at least that’s how it reads to me). \n\nI’m not defending the housing plan or saying it’s perfect, but I worry sometimes that when groups try to be more equitable\/focus on racial justice and then feel overly criticized they abandon those goals altogether because they feel the efforts are thankless. But I suppose that would be an issue for the planning department to work through if they truly are committed to those goals. Anyways, that’s where my brain went "
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
